  "body": "Found during the review of PR #863 (#692).\n\n**What happens:** the MCP feature listing reports a face's `facing` from its normal (`crates/app/src/mcp/features.rs`, `facing()`). On the cylinder-boss fixture used by `crates/app/tests/mcp_pick.rs`, BOTH flat caps of the cylinder come back as \"up\". The new test in PR #863 had to pick the bottom cap by its Z height instead (helper `flat_face`).\n\n**Expected:** the top cap reads \"up\", the bottom cap \"down\".\n\n**Why it matters:** an agent driving CamCraft through MCP chooses faces by `facing` for side-dependent work - `locate_part` mates, `lay_part_on_points`, work-offset picks, op geometry. A bottom face reported as \"up\" can put a part on the wrong side with no refusal.\n\n**To find out first:** whether the fixture's STL has its bottom cap wound upward (then the fixture is wrong and a test with a correctly wound model must show \"down\"), or `facing()` does not take the normal from the facet winding / takes its absolute value (then the tool is wrong). Check with a model whose winding is known to be right (a box from the test helpers, and an imported STEP).\n\nDone means: for a closed solid with correct winding, a downward-facing flat face reads \"down\" in every MCP tool that reports `facing`, pinned by a test with a cylinder and a box; if the fixture's winding was the cause, the fixture is fixed and the height-only `flat_face` helper in `mcp_pick.rs` can go back to using `facing`.\n",
